WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.410
One should be satisfied with the fact that
we have achieved what we have achieved.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.460
It's not nothing.

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.200
Hi There, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.790
Uh, today I want to talk about
perfectionism in language learning.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.990
If you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.400
the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.660
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:22.709
I do appreciate it.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.220
Now, what do I mean by perfectionism?

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.540
I think I, I could also turn
perfectionism in language learning,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.549
the sort of the desire to perform.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:33.750
How did I do?

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:40.500
I've compared that urge to sort of
be judged or the fear to be judged.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:45.510
It's sort of like a, an Olympic
diver and to me, language learning

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:50.490
is very different from Olympic
diving or any kind of performance.

00:00:52.665 --> 00:00:55.785
We learn languages in
order to communicate.

00:00:56.535 --> 00:01:03.405
So this might mean in order to understand
the movie in order to watch a series on

00:01:03.405 --> 00:01:08.625
Netflix and get a sense of some, maybe
distorted sense of the culture, but

00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:12.884
nevertheless, to enjoy that experience,
to understand, and also to communicate,

00:01:12.884 --> 00:01:19.695
to speak, communicate ideas, communicate
feelings, it's all about communication.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.860
So now of course we would all like
to speak, you know, as well as

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.530
we can, as correctly as we can.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.880
And to that extent, it is useful to
every now and again, study grammar.

00:01:35.679 --> 00:01:39.585
Uh, because there are things when we
just listen and read that we don't notice

00:01:39.585 --> 00:01:41.085
in the language, I've said this before.

00:01:41.265 --> 00:01:43.275
Some things have to be pointed out to us.

00:01:43.485 --> 00:01:46.155
And even after they're pointed out
to us, we still won't get them right.

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:50.535
I mean, again, we've had this experience
of correcting a mistake and making the

00:01:50.535 --> 00:01:52.425
same mistake again and again and again.

00:01:53.085 --> 00:01:56.925
And the reason that happens is because
in my mind, the process of learning

00:01:56.925 --> 00:02:03.465
a language is a process of creating
new neural connections in the brain.

00:02:04.485 --> 00:02:10.365
Uh, the brain establishing some patterns,
uh, us slowly getting used to this new

00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:16.155
language, but we get used to it, or we get
accustomed to it in a very individual way.

00:02:16.365 --> 00:02:19.335
It's very much influenced
by our native language.

00:02:19.755 --> 00:02:26.385
Uh, and so some people are going to,
you know, acquire a, a series of habits

00:02:26.385 --> 00:02:30.855
in the language that might be, uh,
with better pronunciation or worst

00:02:30.855 --> 00:02:35.924
pronunciation, or more grammatically
correct or less grammatically correct.

00:02:36.135 --> 00:02:40.424
While we want to get better, we
shouldn't be so worried about how

00:02:40.424 --> 00:02:44.685
we're doing, worried about how
we're being judged that we miss the

00:02:44.685 --> 00:02:47.144
main goal, which is communication.

00:02:48.105 --> 00:02:54.405
Uh, and, and I, the reason I bring this up
right now is becasue amongst the comments

00:02:54.405 --> 00:02:56.565
here, someone said, oh, Steve, you know...

00:02:56.655 --> 00:02:57.885
in fact I get those quite often...

00:02:58.035 --> 00:02:59.685
you say you speak 20 languages.

00:02:59.895 --> 00:03:01.065
In fact, you don't.

00:03:01.275 --> 00:03:05.535
The other day you spoke Italian and you
were very rusty and so forth and so on.

00:03:06.165 --> 00:03:06.495
True.

00:03:06.675 --> 00:03:09.855
And there are languages that I have
spoken and I would have trouble

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:15.105
speaking right now, at least initially,
but those are still languages that

00:03:15.105 --> 00:03:17.475
I have invested a lot of time into.

00:03:18.644 --> 00:03:22.454
Those are languages, like say Korean,
where I'm not at all happy at my

00:03:22.454 --> 00:03:25.994
ability to produce the language or
even to understand the language.

00:03:26.204 --> 00:03:31.424
I have, at least on our count at
LingQ a large, uh, passive vocabulary.

00:03:31.515 --> 00:03:35.894
I understand how the language works, the
structures in the language, because I've

00:03:35.894 --> 00:03:40.035
seen these patterns so many, many times,
but I haven't been using it and therefore

00:03:40.035 --> 00:03:41.894
I can't speak it very well if at all.

00:03:42.644 --> 00:03:44.274
Uh, well I can speak
it, but not very well.

00:03:45.584 --> 00:03:49.600
And, uh, but if I were to go to Korea,
it wouldn't take me a day or two before

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.350
I would be able to speak fairly well.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:57.120
When I was in Greece or Prague or
Romania, I was also communicating.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.640
Communicating, not
perfectly, but communicating.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:06.989
So languages that we learn to a degree,
which is less than perfect, those are

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:11.190
still languages that we have learned
to some extent, we have enjoyed

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.650
learning, we have discovered bits of.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:19.769
And if that is true for languages that I
don't speak as well as I would like, say

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:22.380
Italian, which might be my 10th language.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.360
It's also true for someone who is
learning their first foreign language.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:32.070
In other words, it's always not
quite as good as we would like.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.640
However, it's very much a personal thing.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.610
It's your foreign language.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.720
It's your English, it's your
Italian, it's your Spanish.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.370
And one should be satisfied
with the fact that we have

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:48.930
achieved what we have achieved.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.800
It's not nothing, even though
we would all like to improve.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:57.120
And that's another reason why
we've had this debate before here

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.910
about pitch accent in Japanese.

00:04:59.385 --> 00:05:01.125
I lived in Japan for nine years.

00:05:01.125 --> 00:05:04.365
I've been speaking
Japanese for 40, 50 years.

00:05:05.025 --> 00:05:08.985
Never even knew until recently that
there was such a thing as pitch accent.

00:05:09.105 --> 00:05:11.565
It never prevented me from communicating.

00:05:11.835 --> 00:05:16.755
I do not believe the Japanese
people by and large are bothered

00:05:16.965 --> 00:05:21.405
by someone who has improper pitch
accent, whatever that might mean.

00:05:22.035 --> 00:05:26.325
Um, we don't need to seek perfection.

00:05:26.355 --> 00:05:27.225
We don't need to.

00:05:27.375 --> 00:05:31.035
There's nothing wrong with
aspiring to achieve perfection.

00:05:31.215 --> 00:05:35.985
There's nothing wrong with focusing
on one language and trying to be as

00:05:35.985 --> 00:05:40.035
accurate with the grammar as possible,
trying to get pitch accent, write

00:05:40.035 --> 00:05:44.025
in Japanese and all of these other
things, but it's not necessary.

00:05:44.595 --> 00:05:48.585
And if you don't do those things,
you can still enjoy the language.

00:05:48.615 --> 00:05:54.255
You can still say you speak the
language, uh, because you know,

00:05:54.645 --> 00:06:00.195
performing to a degree of perfection
is a very limited aspect of what's

00:06:00.195 --> 00:06:01.844
involved in learning a language.

00:06:02.415 --> 00:06:08.265
And I think to the extent that people are
concerned about nailing these things down,

00:06:08.565 --> 00:06:14.475
it probably inhibits them and it reduces
the enjoyment of language learning.

00:06:15.795 --> 00:06:20.554
It makes us less likely to engage with
the language, to speak the language.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:24.810
And we all know that the more we
engage with the language, the more we

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:29.520
listen, the more we read, the more we
speak, the more we make mistakes, the

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.140
more we're going to improve in time.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:36.690
So, uh, I just mention that because people
often say, well, you say speak 20...

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.810
You say you speak 20 languages, but
you're not very good at any of them.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:43.200
And yeah, I'm not going to
be as good in 20 languages as

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:44.850
someone who focuses on one.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.380
And there's always people who are
better than me, better than you.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:51.210
It doesn't matter.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:55.770
It doesn't take away from the
enjoyment that we can all achieve in

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.770
learning one or more foreign language.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.100
So that's what I had to say about
the pursuit of perfectionism.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:03.480
Thank you for listening.

